Nominal data → One-Hot Encoding

1. What is Nominal Data?

Nominal data is categorical data where the categories have no natural order or ranking.

Examples from your Insurance dataset

| Column   | Values                                     | Nominal? |
| -------- | ------------------------------------------ | -------- |
| `sex`    | male, female                               | ✅ Yes    |
| `smoker` | yes, no                                    | ✅ Yes    |
| `region` | southwest, southeast, northwest, northeast | ✅ Yes    |

====

or example:

Region:
South → North → East → West

There is no ranking such as:

North > South
East > West

So, this is nominal data.

====


2. Why can't we directly give text to the ML model?

Your Random Forest model needs numerical values.

For example:

sex
-----
male
female
male

The model cannot directly process these text values.

So we use One-Hot Encoding.

====

3. One-Hot Encoding Example

Original nominal data:

| sex    |
| ------ |
| Male   |
| Female |
| Male   |


====

After One-Hot Encoding:

| sex_Male | sex_Female |
| -------- | ---------- |
| 1        | 0          |
| 0        | 1          |
| 1        | 0          |


Each category becomes a separate 0 or 1 column.

====

4. Your code
X = pd.get_dummies(X, drop_first=True)

This performs One-Hot Encoding.

Because you used:

drop_first=True

Pandas removes one category.

Example: sex

Instead of:

| sex_Female | sex_Male |
| ---------- | -------- |
| 1          | 0        |
| 0          | 1        |


====

It may become:

| sex_male |
| -------- |
| 0        |
| 1        |


====

Here:

0 → Female
1 → Male

====

5. region example

Original:

| region    |
| --------- |
| northeast |
| northwest |
| southeast |
| southwest |


====

After:

pd.get_dummies(X, drop_first=True)

You may get:

| region_northwest | region_southeast | region_southwest |
| ---------------- | ---------------- | ---------------- |
| 0                | 0                | 0                |
| 1                | 0                | 0                |
| 0                | 1                | 0                |
| 0                | 0                | 1                |

====

The missing combination:

0, 0, 0

represents the dropped category:

northeast

===

Simple flow
Nominal Data
     ↓
Male / Female
Yes / No
North / South / East / West
     ↓
One-Hot Encoding
     ↓
0 and 1 Numerical Columns
     ↓
Machine Learning Model

---

In your project
sex, smoker, region
        ↓
Nominal Categorical Data
        ↓
pd.get_dummies()
        ↓
One-Hot Encoding
        ↓
RandomForestRegressor
        ↓
Predict Insurance Charges

In short: Nominal data has categories without any order, and One-Hot Encoding converts those categories into numerical 0/1 columns so the machine learning model can use them.

